---@class aws.gamelift.CreateGameSessionInput
---@field FleetId? aws.gamelift.FleetIdOrArn A unique identifier for the fleet to create a game session in. You can use either the fleet ID or ARN value. Each request must reference either a fleet ID or alias ID, but not both.
---@field AliasId? aws.gamelift.AliasIdOrArn A unique identifier for the alias associated with the fleet to create a game session in. You can use either the alias ID or ARN value. Each request must reference either a fleet ID or alias ID, but not both.
---@field MaximumPlayerSessionCount aws.gamelift.WholeNumber The maximum number of players that can be connected simultaneously to the game session.
---@field Name? aws.gamelift.NonZeroAndMaxString A descriptive label that is associated with a game session. Session names do not need to be unique.
---@field GameProperties? aws.gamelift.GamePropertyList A set of key-value pairs that can store custom data in a game session. For example: {"Key": "difficulty", "Value": "novice"}. For an example, see Create a game session with custom properties.
---@field CreatorId? aws.gamelift.NonZeroAndMaxString A unique identifier for a player or entity creating the game session.
---@field GameSessionId? aws.gamelift.IdStringModel This parameter is deprecated. Use IdempotencyToken instead.
---@field IdempotencyToken? aws.gamelift.IdStringModel Custom string that uniquely identifies the new game session request. This is useful for ensuring that game session requests with the same idempotency token are processed only once. Subsequent requests with the same string return the original GameSession object, with an updated status. Maximum token length is 48 characters. If provided, this string is included in the new game session's ID. The valu...
---@field GameSessionData? aws.gamelift.LargeGameSessionData|distilled.Secret A set of custom game session properties, formatted as a single string value. This data is passed to a game server process with a request to start a new game session. For more information, see Start a game session.
---@field Location? aws.gamelift.LocationStringModel A fleet's remote location to place the new game session in. If this parameter is not set, the new game session is placed in the fleet's home Region. Specify a remote location with an Amazon Web Services Region code such as us-west-2. When using an Anywhere fleet, this parameter is required and must be set to the Anywhere fleet's custom location.

---@class aws.gamelift.CreateGameSessionOutput
---@field GameSession? aws.gamelift.GameSession Object that describes the newly created game session record.

---@class aws.gamelift.CreateGameSessionQueueInput
---@field Name aws.gamelift.GameSessionQueueName A descriptive label that is associated with game session queue. Queue names must be unique within each Region.
---@field TimeoutInSeconds? aws.gamelift.WholeNumber The maximum time, in seconds, that a new game session placement request remains in the queue. When a request exceeds this time, the game session placement changes to a TIMED_OUT status. If you don't specify a request timeout, the queue uses a default value.
---@field PlayerLatencyPolicies? aws.gamelift.PlayerLatencyPolicyList A set of policies that enforce a sliding cap on player latency when processing game sessions placement requests. Use multiple policies to gradually relax the cap over time if Amazon GameLift Servers can't make a placement. Policies are evaluated in order starting with the lowest maximum latency value.
---@field Destinations? aws.gamelift.GameSessionQueueDestinationList A list of fleets and/or fleet aliases that can be used to fulfill game session placement requests in the queue. Destinations are identified by either a fleet ARN or a fleet alias ARN, and are listed in order of placement preference.
---@field FilterConfiguration? aws.gamelift.FilterConfiguration A list of locations where a queue is allowed to place new game sessions. Locations are specified in the form of Amazon Web Services Region codes, such as us-west-2. If this parameter is not set, game sessions can be placed in any queue location.
---@field PriorityConfiguration? aws.gamelift.PriorityConfiguration Custom settings to use when prioritizing destinations and locations for game session placements. This configuration replaces the default prioritization process. Priority types that are not explicitly named will be automatically applied at the end of the prioritization process.
---@field CustomEventData? aws.gamelift.QueueCustomEventData|distilled.Secret Information to be added to all events that are related to this game session queue.
---@field NotificationTarget? aws.gamelift.QueueSnsArnStringModel An SNS topic ARN that is set up to receive game session placement notifications. See Setting up notifications for game session placement.
---@field Tags? aws.gamelift.TagList A list of labels to assign to the new game session queue resource. Tags are developer-defined key-value pairs. Tagging Amazon Web Services resources is useful for resource management, access management and cost allocation. For more information, see Tagging Amazon Web Services Resources in the Amazon Web Services General Reference.
---@field LogConfiguration? aws.gamelift.GameSessionQueueLogConfiguration An optional configuration that delivers game session placement insight logs to one or more destinations of your choice. Use these logs to diagnose why a game session placement is FULFILLED, CANCELLED, TIMED_OUT, or FAILED, which locations were evaluated, and the latency and cost factors that influenced each decision.

---@class aws.gamelift.CreateGameSessionQueueOutput
---@field GameSessionQueue? aws.gamelift.GameSessionQueue An object that describes the newly created game session queue.

